Filter java.util.Collection in Java 

I wrote a util class to filter elements in `java.util.Collection` as follows:


    public class Util{
    public static  void filter(Collection l, Filter filter) {
    Iterator it= l.iterator();
    while(it.hasNext()) {
    if(!filter.match(it.next())) {
    it.remove();
    }
    }
    }
    }
    public interface Filter {
    public boolean match(T o);
    }


Questions:

  1. Do you think it's necessary to write the method?
  2. Any improvement about the method?



You should allow any `Filter` not just `Filter`.

Clients might also want to have a method that returns a new Collection instead:


    public static  Collection filter(Collection\\\\xc2\\\\xa0unfiltered, 
    Filter\\\\xc2\\\\xa0filter)

split a java collection into sub collections based on a object property 

I have a List of MyObjects ... MyObject{ int id,String name}. Now I want to split the the list into sublists that have identical "id" values, can any one suggest an efficient approach for doing this. 


    // create the thing to store the sub lists
    Map> subs = new HashMap>();
    // iterate through your objects
    for(MyObject o : list){
    // fetch the list for this object's id
    List temp = subs.get(o.getId());
    if(temp == null){
    // if the list is null we haven't seen an
    // object with this id before, so create 
    // a new list
    temp = new ArrayList();
    // and add it to the map
    subs.put(o.getId(), temp);
    }
    // whether we got the list from the map
    // or made a new one we need to add our
    // object.
    temp.add(o);
    }

