Generic method to manage a map of collections 

In the application I'm developing, I make use of several maps associating strings to collections of elements, e.g. `Map>`, `Map>`. In many cases I want simple functions to add/remove elements in the collection given by a certain key, possibly removing or creating new entries in the map.

I implemented some generic methods for the effect, but the `putIntoCollection()` method is giving me some problems. My implementation, which does not rise any warning, is the following:


    public static > void putIntoCollection(
    Map map, K key, V value, Class collectionClass)
    throws InstantiationException, IllegalAccessException {
    C collection = map.get(key);
    if (collection == null) {
    collection = collectionClass.newInstance();
    map.put(key, collection);
    }
    collection.add(value);
    }


`C` represents the type of the collection, which can be any type of `Collection`, and the `Class` parameter allows me to pass a concrete class token to instantiate new `C`'s (e.g. pass a token of `ArrayList` for a Map of `Lists`).

However, if I try to use it like this:


    Map> tags;
    String key, value;
    MapUtilities.putIntoCollection(map, key, value, HashSet.class);


I get the compile error:


    The parameterized method >putIntoCollection(Map>, K, V, Class>) of type MapUtilities is not applicable for the arguments (Map>, K, V, Class) 


I understand that happens because I'm passing a `Class` argument while it expects a parametrized Set class. However, I don't know how (or if) can I obtain such instances of `Class`. Is there a better way to do a generic method such as this?

Are you in a position to use third-party libraries? You're basically reinventing Guava's `Multimap` \-- `ListMultimap` and `SortedSetMultimap` are your two examples. A bunch of implementations are provided -- most notably, for your case, `ArrayListMultimap` and `TreeMultimap`.

That said, it's generally easier to just pass an explicit factory object:


    interface Supplier { 
    T get();
    }
    void putIntoCollection(Map>, K, V, Supplier> emptySetSupplier);

Generic method to manage a map of collections 

In the application I'm developing, I make use of several maps associating strings to collections of elements, e.g. `Map>`, `Map>`. In many cases I want simple functions to add/remove elements in the collection given by a certain key, possibly removing or creating new entries in the map.

I implemented some generic methods for the effect, but the `putIntoCollection()` method is giving me some problems. My implementation, which does not rise any warning, is the following:


    public static > void putIntoCollection(
    Map map, K key, V value, Class collectionClass)
    throws InstantiationException, IllegalAccessException {
    C collection = map.get(key);
    if (collection == null) {
    collection = collectionClass.newInstance();
    map.put(key, collection);
    }
    collection.add(value);
    }


`C` represents the type of the collection, which can be any type of `Collection`, and the `Class` parameter allows me to pass a concrete class token to instantiate new `C`'s (e.g. pass a token of `ArrayList` for a Map of `Lists`).

However, if I try to use it like this:


    Map> tags;
    String key, value;
    MapUtilities.putIntoCollection(map, key, value, HashSet.class);


I get the compile error:


    The parameterized method >putIntoCollection(Map>, K, V, Class>) of type MapUtilities is not applicable for the arguments (Map>, K, V, Class) 


I understand that happens because I'm passing a `Class` argument while it expects a parametrized Set class. However, I don't know how (or if) can I obtain such instances of `Class`. Is there a better way to do a generic method such as this?

Your approach can't work because `Class` is not a sub-class of `Class`. So you won't be able to use an instance of `Class` or any other container that carries the type of interest (`? extends C`) as a generic type. You can only use `C` as a direct parameter, like a kind of prototype.

Look at ` T[] java.util.List.toArray(T[] a)` as an example. This method also just wants to know the type `T` of the resulting array. It can't be delivered as `Class` so the method wants the client to give an instance.

comparing two collections for comparing two text files for additions, deletions, modifications 

I have two collections as below which hold IDs for Students.

The ids are Strings in the format 111-1111. e.g. of ids 221-2534, 215-6365, etc.


     Collection newKeys = new ArrayList();
    Collection oldKeys = new ArrayList();


The ids are in a fixed format file along with other data. That is first 8 char ids, next 10 char name, next 10 char addr, etc.

I am reading ids into collection as below:


    String oldFile = "C:\\\\oldFile.dat";
    String newFile = "C:\\\\newFile.dat";
    BufferedReader in;
    String str;
    // Read keys from old file
    in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(oldFile));
    while ((str = in.readLine()) != null) {
    oldKeys.add(str.substring(0, 8).trim());
    }
    in.close();
    // Read keys from new file
    in = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(newFile));
    while ((str = in.readLine()) != null) {
    newKeys.add(str.substring(0, 8).trim());
    }
    in.close(); 


Here the entries in the file are sorted on SSN. So I believe the collections formed will also be sorted.

Now:

Case: I want to know the differences as resultant lists by comparing the two collections. That is I need lists which contains entries which got added, entries which got removed and entries which are same.

I will then use the list having common entries to read corresponding data from both files and compare that for any modifications.

That is after I have the common list -- 

a) Take a id from the list. Read the corresponding data for this id from both files into Strings. Compare the String for any differences. In case of a difference, move the newFile String into a fileWithUpdates.

b) Do nothing in case of no difference.

Questions:

1) Is this correct approach ? 

2) Also how to compare the two collections to get resultant lists viz. toBeDeleted, toBeAdded and sameEntries ?

3) How to read a specific line from a file on a key (student id in this case) ?

Update:

Based on below answer, added the below code:


    Iterator iOld = oldKeys.iterator();
    Iterator iNew = newKeys.iterator();
    Map tempMap = new HashMap();
    while (iOld.hasNext()) {
    tempMap.put(iOld.next(), "old");
    }
    while (iNew.hasNext()) {
    String temp = iNew.next();
    if (tempMap.containsKey(temp)) {
    tempMap.put(temp, "both");
    }
    else {
    System.out.println("here");
    tempMap.put(temp, "new");
    }
    }


So now I have a map which has:

Entries to be compared: Entries in above map with value "both"

Entries to be added: Entries in above map with value "new"

Entries to be deleted: Entries in above map with value "old" 

So my problem boils down to:

How to read a specific line from a file on a key so that I can compare them for data modifications?? 

Thanks for reading!

you could proceed like this,


    Collection newKeys = new ArrayList(); 
    Collection oldKeys = new ArrayList(); 
    Collection toBeDeleted = new ArrayList(oldKeys).removeAll(newKeys);
    Collection toBeAdded = new ArrayList(newKeys).removeAll(oldKeys);
    Collection sameEntries = new ArrayList(newKeys).removeAll(toBeAdded);


though for the third question, you would be better off with using a HashMap (or a TreeMap if you want to keep the keys automatically sorted).

***updates

In your original file reading code, you can make the following change,


    Map oldContentMap = new HashMap(); 
    while ((str = in.readLine()) != null) { 
    oldKeys.add(str.substring(0, 8).trim()); 
    oldContentMap.put(str.substring(0, 8).trim(),str.substring(8).trim());
    } 
    in.close(); 


and similarly for new file,


     Map newContentMap = new HashMap(); 
    while ((str = in.readLine()) != null) { 
    newKeys.add(str.substring(0, 8).trim()); 
    newContentMap.put(str.substring(0, 8).trim(),str.substring(8).trim());
    } 
    in.close(); 


Now you can proceed to compare by,


    for (Map.Entry entry : tempMap.entrySet()) { 
    if(entry.getValue().equals("both"){ //comparing for keys in both lists
    String oldContent = oldContentMap.get(entry.getKey());
    String newContent = newContentMap.get(entry.getKey());
    if(oldContent.equals(newContent)){
    System.out.println("Different data for key:"+entry.getKey());
    }
    }
    }


you can use necessary temp variable and move the declarations outside the loop as well..

