Java - Better model to save objects (Collection) 

Consider the below case

1) I need to save objects of Location class (defined in my app) along with all the User (defined in my app) objects as collection as shown below


    HashMap>


And the data is stored like below in HashMap (as objects, i am mentioning name for explanation)


    Location1 - User1, User2, User3
    Location2 - User2, User3
    Location3 - User1


Now in the above example, in hashmap redundant copies of User1, User2 and User3 are stored inside each Location. is this correct method to save data of one to many relation in a collection? 

There is no need of `HashMap`.You can have `User` contained in `Location` class as it suits your requirement perfectly.


    public class Location {
    private Set users = new HashSet();
    public boolean addUser(User user) {
    return users.add(user);
    }
    public boolean remove(User user) {
    return users.remove(user);
    }
    public Set getAll() {
    return Collections.unmodifiableSet(users);
    }
    }

Java - Better model to save objects (Collection) 

Consider the below case

1) I need to save objects of Location class (defined in my app) along with all the User (defined in my app) objects as collection as shown below


    HashMap>


And the data is stored like below in HashMap (as objects, i am mentioning name for explanation)


    Location1 - User1, User2, User3
    Location2 - User2, User3
    Location3 - User1


Now in the above example, in hashmap redundant copies of User1, User2 and User3 are stored inside each Location. is this correct method to save data of one to many relation in a collection? 


    public class Location {
    private Set users = new HashSet();
    public boolean addUser(User user) {
    return users.add(user);
    }
    public boolean remove(User user) {
    return users.remove(user);
    }
    public Set getAll() {
    return Collections.unmodifiableSet(users);
    }
    }


Identical to Amit's answer just keep a `Set` associated with your `Location`. Then, you should additionally use an `ArrayList` to store all of your `Locations`. As Arjun has pointed out, there is no redundancy (duplicated User Objects). When will there be no redundancy? When each `User` Object is only created once, and then added to multiple Collections. This is because the reference to the `User` is actually being stored in the Collection. That is what is meant in Java by "pass a reference by value". In a List's add method, for example, you may call `add(User)` but what Java does in the background is passes a copy of the reference (think memory location) of the `User` Object to the List. So the List doesn't actually store the User Object directly, however, it is located in memory somewhere.

