    }
    // ...
    }


Problematic subclass:


    public class ListColumn, T> extends Column
    { 
    public ListColumn(String name)
    {
    super((Class) List.class, name);
    }
    // ...
    }


The problematic subclass (`ListColumn`) compiles fine in Eclipse, with Java compiler compliance set to 1.6. However, when compiled through Maven (using the JDK compiler, with source and target set to 1.6), I get an `Inconvertible types` error at the `super()` call in the `ListColumn` constructor.

Edit: Eclipse does warn about this line (`Type safety: Unchecked cast from Class to Class`).

I know that Eclipse does not use the JDL compiler, and apparently the compiler it does use is less strict than the JDK one.

However, my question is how can I make this code work on the 1.6 JDK compiler.

In other words, how can a I call the super constructor with an argument that conforms to the expected `Class` type where `L` is restricted to `extends List`.

Preferably I only want to make changes in the `ListColumn` constructor, not in the `Column` class.

Assume I instantiate your class as follows:


    ListColumn, String> listColumn = new ListColumn, String>("column");


Now, your code will try to cast `List.class` to a `Class` which is not the case.

You can make use of covariance in your `Column` class if you want to allow clients to use subtypes of your `T` generic type. For instance, changing the `Column` constructor to:


    public Column(Class type, String name)


would allow you to call super in your `ListColumn` without any cast, since any subtype of `List` would now be allowed, but in a type safe manner.

As for tricking the compiler to accept any sort of generics cast with a mere warning, you can use the most dirty "double cast" trick that simply eliminates generics so the compiler won't catch up with your code anymore:


    List l = new ArrayList();
    List s = (List) (List) l;


But is that really what you want ?

What's the differences between Array declaration and Collection declaration 


     1. List carList = new ArrayList();
    2. List carList = new ArrayList();
    carList.add(new Sedan());


1 has a compiler error and 2 is legal.

Why the type of variable declaration must match the type we pass to the object type(derived type is not allowed)? I was using Array as following which absolutely correct:


    int SIZE = 10;
    Car[] carArray = new Sedan[SIZE];


Can anyone tell me why collections must declare as condition 2? Thanks

`Sedan[]` is a sub-type of `Car[]`. But this has a very negative impact on type safety, because the following code is thus legal:


    Sedan[] sedans = new Sedan[10];
    Car[] cars = sedans; // legal, since Sedan[] extends Car[]
    cars[0] = new Car(); // Houston, we have a problem


This code causes an ArrayStoreException at runtime, because an array supposed to only contain sedans would contain a non-Sedan car.

The goal of generics was to have type-safe collections. And the decision was thus taken to design them so that the above problem would be detected at compile time, rather than runtime. Collections are much more used than arrays, and it's quite a good thing to have them type-safe.

So they decided that a `List` would not be a `List`.

