As for the property "sorted", filters according to 


    public Collection someFilter(Collection input)


might be written in a way that permits you to guarantee that the property "sorted" is not violated, i.e., the filter is "stable".

For instance, iterating the input collection and storing whatever passes the filter in an ArrayList and returning that will be "stable".

(Of course, there may be transformation where you cannot guarantee that.)

Another property would be the preservation of "object identity" as opposed to copying, but this is a little more esoteric ;-)

return duplicates from an array in efficient way in java 

I want to return duplicates in an array.


    int[] strArray = new int[] {1,1, 2, 3, 2, 2, 3};


I have used below method to return duplicates.


    private static Set checkDuplicate(int[] intArray) {
    Set values = new HashSet<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < intArray.length - 1; i++) {
    if (intArray[i] == (intArray[i + 1])) {
    values.add(intArray[i]);
    }
    else
    System.out.println("not equal");
    }
    return values;
    }


But in this way it checks only the consequtive values.And this needs huge comparisons and time consuming. So is there any better way to do this?

If efficiency is what you are looking for then use `HashMap` that it has `O(1) speed`, Also iterate the array of integers in `enhanced forloop` because it is slightly faster than ordinary `forloop`


     private static Set checkDuplicate(int[] intArray) {
    HashMap values = new HashMap();
    Set values2 = new HashSet();
    for(Integer i : intArray) //0(n)
    {
    if(values.get(i) != null) //O(1)
    values2.add(i);
    else
    values.put(i, i);
    }
    return values2;
    }

