Overload java method by List<> 

I have the code, where methods is overlays with `List<>` arguments 


    RetrunType1 func(List arg);
    ReturnType2 func(List arg);


and Type1!=Type2, but that code compile and work fine on jdk1.6.0_45. I know that this sample don't compile and work. How I can understand that? 

The problem here is something called type erasure which you can read about here. The short version is however, that the Java compiler will remove the generic arguments so as to make the byte code compatible to previous Java versions. So both methods have the signature


    RetrunType1 func(List arg);
    ReturnType2 func(List arg);


So to Java those functions look pretty much the same.

Compile time error when adding an Integer to a generic ArrayList of String 

I know this question has been asked multiple times before but I'm looking for an answer based on type erasure.

Why does the compiler give an error on adding `Integer` to `ArrayList`? I want to understand this by type erasure and byte code of `add` method in `ArrayList`. 

Let's start with a small example (note this uses raw types; and they are evil),


    public static void main(String[] args) {
    List strings = Arrays.asList("Hello");
    List al = new ArrayList(strings);
    for (Integer v : al) {
    System.out.println(v);
    }
    }


It compiles. If we run it we get


    Exception in thread "main" java.lang.ClassCastException: java.lang.String cannot be cast to java.lang.Integer
    at com.stackoverflow.Example.main(Example.java:11)


Because `String` is not `Integer`. We can examine the bytecode with `javap -v`


     35: invokeinterface #37, 1 // InterfaceMethod java/util/Iterator.next:()Ljava/lang/Object;
    40: checkcast #43 // class java/lang/Integer
    43: astore_3
    44: getstatic #45 // Field java/lang/System.out:Ljava/io/PrintStream;


And see that the cast fails on the line numbered 40. Generic types are for compile time type checking, at run-time (almost) everything is an `Object`.

