    118 -- this has priority than any lstr2
    120 -- this has the lowest priority
    */
    // use case 2 with some cases with similar priorities 
    }
    }


Not entirely clear how the rules for combining priority are, but I suggest you just try to add up all the priorities the items have in the different lists and sort them by that combined priority.


    public static  List priorize(Map, Integer> listsToPriority) {
    Map totalPriority = new HashMap();
    List allElements = new ArrayList<>();
    for (List list : listsToPriority.keySet()) {
    int priority = listsToPriority.get(list);
    for (T x : list) {
    if (totalPriority.containsKey(x)) {
    totalPriority.put(x, totalPriority.get(x) + priority);
    } else {
    totalPriority.put(x, priority);
    allElements.add(x);
    }
    }
    }
    // sort by total priority in reverse order
    allElements.sort(new Comparator () {
    public int compare(T x, T y) {
    return totalPriority.get(y).compareTo(totalPriority.get(x)); 
    }
    });
    return allElements;
    }


Usage:


    Map, Integer> priorizedLists = new HashMap<>();
    priorizedLists.put(lStr, 3); // 3 == high priority
    priorizedLists.put(lStr2, 2);
    priorizedLists.put(lStr3, 1); // 1 == low priority
    priorizedLists.put(lStr4, 2);
    priorizedLists.put(lStr5, 3);
    List priorized = priorize(priorizedLists);
    System.out.println(priorized);


Output:


    [113, 112, 111, 119, 114, 115, 118, 120]


Some notes:

  * For this approach, items with high priority should actually have a high priority number, i.e. what has priority 1 in your example, has priority 3 in mine, but this should be easy to translate.
  * The output does not exactly match your output, but as I said, the rules for combining priorities are not 100% clear. You can replace the `+` with `*` to compute the product of all the priorities; this will in fact get the result that you want, but I do not know whether this makes sense in your setting.

Java - Create sublist from a list based on suffix I'm trying to create sublists from a list based on the suffix.


    public class Test {
    public static void main(String args[]) {
    List> subList = new ArrayList>();
    List myList = new ArrayList();
    myList.add("Text_1");
    myList.add("XYZ_3");
    myList.add("ABC_1");
    myList.add("Text_2");
    myList.add("Text_3");
    myList.add("XYZ_1");
    myList.add("XYZ_2");
    myList.add("ABC_2");
    for (String item : myList) {
    List tempList = new ArrayList();
    String suffix = item.substring(item.lastIndexOf("_"));
    tempList.add(item);
    for (String value : myList) {
    if (value.endsWith(suffix) && !tempList.contains(value)) {
    tempList.add(value);
    }
    }
    System.out.println(tempList);
    }
    }
    }


I'm expecting like below


    // Text_1, ABC_1, XYZ_1
    // Text_2, ABC_2, XYZ_2
    // Text_3, XYZ_3


But the actual is


    [Text_1, ABC_1, XYZ_1]
    [XYZ_3, Text_3]
    [ABC_1, Text_1, XYZ_1]
    [Text_2, XYZ_2, ABC_2]
    [Text_3, XYZ_3]
    [XYZ_1, Text_1, ABC_1]
    [XYZ_2, Text_2, ABC_2]
    [ABC_2, Text_2, XYZ_2]


Any help is appreciated. Thanks There are several problems with your code: 1) you build the tempList on every item, so while the result are correct, you will see them for every item in myList. you need to "remember" each suffix you process so that you won't process it again. a `HashMap` will do the job 2) if you want tempList to have uniqe values, use `Set` here is the complete solution 


     public static void main(String args[]) {
    Map> subList = new HashMap<>();
    List myList = new ArrayList();
    myList.add("Text_1");
    myList.add("XYZ_3");
    myList.add("ABC_1");
    myList.add("Text_2");
    myList.add("Text_3");
    myList.add("XYZ_1");
    myList.add("XYZ_2");
    myList.add("ABC_2");
    for (String item : myList) {
    String suffix = item.substring(item.lastIndexOf("_"));
    if (subList.containsKey(suffix)) continue;
    Set tempSet = new HashSet();
    tempSet.add(item);
    for (String value : myList) {
    if (value.endsWith(suffix)) {
    tempSet.add(value);
    }
    }
    subList.put(suffix, tempSet);
    }
    System.out.println(subList);
    }


output:


    {_1=[Text_1, ABC_1, XYZ_1], _2=[ABC_2, XYZ_2, Text_2], _3=[XYZ_3, Text_3]}

