What is the difference between HashMap and HashMap<>? What is the difference between 


    Map map = new HashMap();


and 


    Map map = new HashMap<>();


Which is better for use and why? Really it should be `Map map = new HashMap<>();` The 2 fields in the `<>` represent the key and value type of the map. So in this example it's a map of Strings which a referenced by Long values. Including them in the declaration forces the Map to ensure that the map can only have specific Key/Value types. It used to be that the types had to be included in the `<>` of the assignment but it hasn't been needed since Java 7.

