Filtering collection to get items whose properties occured n times in Java 8 using streams Assume that I have a Collection of Person(name, age) objects:


    Set people = new HashSet();
    people.add(new Person("Peter", 26));
    people.add(new Person("Dane", 24));
    people.add(new Person("Gregory", 26));
    people.add(new Person("Daniel", 27));
    people.add(new Person("Micheal", 27));
    people.add(new Person("David", 26));
    people.add(new Person("Kate", 21));
    people.forEach(i -> System.out.println(i.getName()));


now I would like to check if there are three guys who have the same age or, if there are pairs of people with the same name, and get these people in another list. So I wrote following generic static method:


    private static  Collection filterWhenMultipleOccurance(Collection collection,
    Function comparisonCriteria, int demandedOccuranceAmount) {
    return collection.stream()
    .filter(item -> collection.stream()
    .filter(otherItem -> 
    comparisonCriteria.apply(otherItem) == comparisonCriteria.apply(item))
    .toArray().length == demandedOccuranceAmount)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
    }


Now I can call this method to meet my demands:


    Collection selectedPeople = filterWhenMultipleOccurance(people, p -> p.getAge(), 3);
    System.out.println("---------------");
    selectedPeople.forEach(p -> System.out.println(p.getName()));


Which gets me proper list consisting of Peter, Gregory and David. Somehow I feel this solution is neither elegant nor efficient, since I'm nesting stream().filter call, so I assume that time complexity would be at least O(n^2). Is there a better solution to this problem? I think the simplest solution would be to group the entries into buckets with your given bucketing criteria and then return the list which satisfies your requirement. Something along the lines of:


    public class StackOverflow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
    Set people = new HashSet();
    people.add(new Person("Peter", 26));
    people.add(new Person("Dane", 24));
    people.add(new Person("Gregory", 26));
    people.add(new Person("Daniel", 27));
    people.add(new Person("Micheal", 27));
    people.add(new Person("David", 26));
    people.add(new Person("Kate", 21));
    Collection lst = filterWhenMultipleOccurance(people, Person::getAge, 3);
    System.out.println(lst);
    }
    public static  Collection filterWhenMultipleOccurance(Collection collection,
    Function comparisonCriteria, int demandedOccuranceAmount) {
    Map> grps = collection.stream().collect(Collectors.groupingBy(comparisonCriteria));
    for(List lst : grps.values()) {
    if(lst.size() >= demandedOccuranceAmount) {
    return lst;
    }
    }
    return Collections.emptyList();
    }
    }


Not really jazzy or compact but does the job.

