> If there are more properties, maybe more than 20, and the constructor can not use directly, how to convert ?

If you have any more than 3-4 properties to set, you should use a Builder, like so:


    List A = ImmutableList.of(new Developer("Ivan", 25),
    new Developer("Curry", 28));
    Function converter = dev -> new ProductManagerBuilder()
    .setAge(dev.getAge())
    .setName(dev.getName())
    .setProperty1(dev.getProperty1())
    .setProperty2(dev.getProperty2())
    ...
    .build();
    List productManagers = A.stream()
    .map(converter)
    .collect(toList());

String list get an item starting without loop I have a array list contains thousands of data. For Example:


    List custNames = new ArrayList();
    custNames.add("John");
    custNames.add("Tom");
    custNames.add("Bart");
    custNames.add("Tim");
    custNames.add("Broad");


Now I want to get count of names only starting with 'T'. I used looping mechanism for my solution.


    List filterNames = new ArrayList();
    String nameStarts="T";
    for(int i=0;i

But I have very large collection of data in this custNames list. Is there any different solution without using loop? Thanks. First, you can shorten your initialization with `Arrays.asList(T)`; Second, I would use a simple loop to build a table of counts once and then use that to determine the subsequent queries. Something like,


    List custNames = new ArrayList(Arrays.asList("John", "Tom", 
    "Bart", "Tim", "Broad"));
    int[] counts = new int[26];
    for (String name : custNames) {
    char ch = Character.toLowerCase(name.charAt(0));
    counts[ch - 'a']++;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < counts.length; i++) {
    if (counts[i] > 0) {
    System.out.printf("There are %d words that start with %c%n", 
    counts[i], (char) ('a' + i));
    }
    }


Which outputs


    There are 2 words that start with b
    There are 1 words that start with j
    There are 2 words that start with t


Or, in the specific case - `counts['t' - 'a']` is the count of words starting with `t`.

