Why Collections.copy operation works on size of list instead of capacity of list? Below is the code, which I tried:


    //Initialized the first list with initial capacity 2
    List list1=new ArrayList(2);
    list1.add("banana");
    list1.add("apple");
    //Initialized the second list with initial capacity 4
    List list2=new ArrayList(4);
    //Performing copying
    Collections.copy(list2, list1);


But copying operation failed with IndexOutOfBoundsOperation.  
Reason :- source does not fit in dest.

> Question why copy operation is working on size i.e. no of elements in a list? Why it doesn't operate on capacity?


    public static  void copy(List dest, List src)


This method is supposed to work for any two `List` implementations. What if you run it with :


    List l1 = Arrays.asList("a","b","c");
    List l2 = Arrays.asList("1","2");
    Collections.copy(l1,l2);


`Collections.copy` can't use `add` to increase the size of `l2`, since `Arrays.asList` returns a fixed size `List` and doesn't support `add`. Therefore the size of `l2` must be large enough to fit all the elements of `l1`. Therefore, looking at the implementation, you'll see that `Collections.copy` uses `set` and not `add`.

Why Collections.copy operation works on size of list instead of capacity of list? Below is the code, which I tried:


    //Initialized the first list with initial capacity 2
    List list1=new ArrayList(2);
    list1.add("banana");
    list1.add("apple");
    //Initialized the second list with initial capacity 4
    List list2=new ArrayList(4);
    //Performing copying
    Collections.copy(list2, list1);


But copying operation failed with IndexOutOfBoundsOperation.  
Reason :- source does not fit in dest.

> Question why copy operation is working on size i.e. no of elements in a list? Why it doesn't operate on capacity?


    public static  void copy(List dest, List src)


This method is supposed to work for any two `List` implementations. What if you run it with :


    List l1 = Arrays.asList("a","b","c");
    List l2 = Arrays.asList("1","2");
    Collections.copy(l1,l2);


`Collections.copy` can't use `add` to increase the size of `l2`, since `Arrays.asList` returns a fixed size `List` and doesn't support `add`. Therefore the size of `l2` must be large enough to fit all the elements of `l1`. Therefore, looking at the implementation, you'll see that `Collections.copy` uses `set` and not `add`.

