For now my code doesn't sort the list when I enter a integer value. Is the way I'm doing that is correct? I found example of Collection through internet, but it doesn't work for me. Further enhancing the suggestion from dimo414. As said, you create a class that contains the relevant pieces for an employee. That class could have a constructor like


    public Employee(String firstName, String lastName, int salary, int experienceInMonths)


and you would then create employees like


    List employees = new ArrayList<>();
    employees.add( new Employee ("Bilbo", "Beutlin", 5, 10) );


But of course, your date is coming from a file, which you say has lines like


    Name: Thomas Green | Gross salary: 10 000 | Experience: 30 months


So, what you do know:

  1. You write code that reads that file line by line
  2. You create a helper method that takes one such line ... and returns an Employee object for that

Like:


    public Employee createEmployeeFrom(String line) {


And within that method, you simply have to parse that string and extract the relevant information. Given your format, you could do:

  * use String.split("\\\|") ... that should slice your line into three substrings 
  * First substring would be "Name: Thomas Green" then. Here you would use String.substring() to acquire everything after ": "; giving you "Thomas Green". Then you could assume that anything after the last space is the last name; and anything before goes as first name
  * Then you do similar things for the parts of your input (using methods such as Integer.valueOf() to turn String numbers into int)
  * Finally, you make a call `return new Employee(firstNameParsedFromInput, .... 

Things is: you have to separate concerns. One concern is modeling your data using an Employee class; the other concern is to parse a string to retrieve the elements that make up an Employee. And only when all of that works nicely, you then start looking into Collections.sort() and writing your own Comparator class.

