The problem is in the `size()` method, you didn't take into consideration two edge cases: 

  1. When the list is empty (you'll get NPE for trying to execute `n.getNext()`
  2. When there's only one Node in the list (it doesn't have "next" so it'll return zero instead of 1)

You can easily fix it by adding the following in the beginning of the method:


    public int size() {
    int size = 1;
    if (firstNode == null) {
    return 0;
    }
    try {...


There is another bug in the method `add`. The for-loop is unnecessarily complicated and doesn't handle some edge cases. Modify it to:


    public void add(int index, Object obj) {
