Java: I have a List of cars that i wanna sort by registrationNumber(String) that is inside one of the elements in the list I have a ist in java, that has car objects inside, and inside one of the car objects is a String thats named RegistrationNumber, and i wanna sort the list by that List cars = new ArrayList();


    public Collection sortedByRegistrationNumber() {
    }


Hi There are two ways to do it.

  1. Using Comparable Interface: In this your bean itself is to be implemented with an interface called comparable and override a method compareTo in which you will write logic on which basis you want classes to compare in your case reg No. The problem is if in future you want to have a sorting on the basis of let us assume make year or anything else you cant do that.

         public class Car implements Comparable{
         private String regNo;
         public Car(String regNo){
         super();
         this.regNo = regNo;
         }
         public String getRegNo(){
         return regNo;
         }
         public void setRegNo(String regNo){
         this.regNo = regNo;
         }
         @Override
         public int compareTo(Car o){ 
         // Here when we compare if both the variables are equal return 0
         if(this.regNo.equals(o.getRegNo())){
         return 0; 
         // If first one is bigger than the latter return 1
         }else if(this.regNo.compareTo(o.getRegNo()) > 0){
         return 1; 
         // else return 0
         }else{
         return -1; 
         }
         } 
         }


To Test:

         Car c1 = new Car("Z");
         Car c2 = new Car("A");
         Car c3 = new Car("C");
         Car c4 = new Car("B");
         List list = new ArrayList<>();
         list.add(c1);
         list.add(c2);
         list.add(c3);
         list.add(c4);
         Collections.sort(list);
         System.out.println(list);


  2. Using Comparator Interface: To remove the Comparable's drawback Comparator comes to the rescue. In comparator you will create a new class called and implement it with Comparator interface. You will have to override the comparator's compare method which takes two arguments which are the objects you want to compare. How this helps is you can have multiple comparators based on the need and you just need to pass the Comparator to the `Collections.sort` method as and argument which will sort it automatically. Below is the example for the same class. Here you dont need to implement the imple,

         public class Car2
         {
         private String regNo;
         public Car2(String regNo){
         super();
         this.regNo = regNo;
         }
         public String getRegNo(){
         return regNo;
         }
         public void setRegNo(String regNo){
         this.regNo = regNo;
         }
         }


Comparator class

         public class CarComparator implements Comparator
         {
         @Override
         public int compare(Car2 o1, Car2 o2)
         {
         Car2 c1 = (Car2) o1;
         Car2 c2 = (Car2) o2;
         //TODO check for nulls
         if(c1.getRegNo().equals(c2.getRegNo())){
         return 0; 
         }else if(c1.getRegNo().compareTo(c2.getRegNo()) > 0){
         return 1; 
         }else{
         return -1; 
         }
         } 
         }


Test

         Car2 c1 = new Car2("Z");
         Car2 c2 = new Car2("A");
         Car2 c3 = new Car2("C");
         Car2 c4 = new Car2("B");
         List list = new ArrayList<>();
         list.add(c1);
         list.add(c2);
         list.add(c3);
         list.add(c4);
         Collections.sort(list,new CarComparator());
         System.out.println(list);



You can find more information here: 1.Comparable 2.Comparator

