What is the difference between Collection.add() method and Collection.addAll() method in Collections in Java I have two ArrayList(a1 and a2) as below :


    ArrayList a1 = new ArrayList();
    a1.add(8);
    a1.add("a1");
    ArrayList a2 = new ArrayList();
    a2.add(a1); //output : [[8, a1]]
    a2.addAll(a1); //output : [[8, a1], 8, a1]


My Question : 1) Why a2.addAll() method prints the data twice? 2) What is the exact difference between add() and addAll()? Both the methods return boolean. `add()` just add to next index the object:


    public boolean add(E arg0) {
    this.ensureCapacityInternal(this.size + 1);
    this.elementData[this.size++] = arg0;
    return true;
    }


`addAll()` copy the values inside the Collection given:


    public boolean addAll(Collection arg0) {
    Object[] arg1 = arg0.toArray();
    int arg2 = arg1.length;
    this.ensureCapacityInternal(this.size + arg2);
    System.arraycopy(arg1, 0, this.elementData, this.size, arg2);
    this.size += arg2;
    return arg2 != 0;
    }

